---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/alternatives/firepush-alternative';
const title = "Firepush alternatives: separate web push from legacy SMS";
const desc = "Compare the current product scope, practical replacement choices and a reversible Shopify handover for Firepush.";
const faqs = [{"question": "Does Firepush currently send SMS?", "answer": "The retrieved official homepage and current web-push comparison describe web push only. Confirm legacy email/SMS account records and access directly with support."}, {"question": "Can I import push subscribers like an email CSV?", "answer": "Do not assume portability. The providers must establish a supported procedure for the origin, service worker, subscription endpoints and credentials, or a new opt-in path."}, {"question": "When should I keep Firepush?", "answer": "Keep it when the current push journeys fit and replacement benefits do not justify subscription continuity risk and operating cost."}, {"question": "What must happen before recovery moves?", "answer": "Assign one owner, stop old enrollments, reconcile pending jobs, and demonstrate current purchase and permission checks with late-event deduplication."}];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a href="/alternatives" class="link">← All alternatives</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Firepush alternatives: separate web push from legacy SMS</h1>
    <div class="prose-shopify mt-8">
      <h2>Current Firepush scope is web push</h2>
      <p>The <a href="https://getfirepush.com/">current Firepush homepage</a> presents Shopify web push, including welcome, cart, price-drop, stock and order notifications. Its <a href="https://getfirepush.com/web-push-apps-compared/">current web-push comparison</a>, retrieved on 25 November 2026, explicitly says Firepush sends web push only. Do not infer present email or SMS service from older branding or historical articles. If your account holds legacy email/SMS records, confirm access and export arrangements with support; this page does not establish their availability.</p>
      <h2>Choose a replacement by channel</h2>
      <p>Stay with Firepush if its web push journeys and operating costs fit. Repair prompt placement or recovery rules if those are the actual problems. For a push replacement, PushOwl is a candidate in Firepush’s comparison; Omnisend is a candidate when you also want to investigate email/SMS consolidation. Confirm capabilities and quotes with each supplier rather than treating a competitor table as a guarantee. For a separate SMS requirement, evaluate a sender against your destinations, replies and Shopify events. Sequenzy is not an automatic replacement for browser push or legacy SMS.</p>
      <h2>Proposed exercise: restock and cart recovery</h2>
      <p>Use a controlled outdoor-goods fixture with a sold-out size, a price drop and a cart that later becomes an order. Include two browsers on one device, a second device, denied permission and a withdrawn subscription. These are proposed acceptance exercises, not observed results. Check which alert follows a product, variant or customer, and what happens if the original variant is still unavailable. Confirm deep links resolve to the intended live product and suppress an obsolete offer after the order or price changes.</p>
      <h2>Inventory records before removing the old integration</h2>
      <p>Separate legacy email addresses, phone numbers, browser subscriptions, campaign logs, order links, consent evidence and suppression records. Identify export rights, retention, access roles and the date archive access will end. Preserve disclosure, purpose, channel, sender, timestamp and source for any email/SMS records. A phone number or a push permission is not SMS authorization. Mark missing evidence for review without enrolling those records. Keep service/order notifications distinct from promotional permissions and establish who owns each destination.</p>
      <h2>Browser permission is not a portable contact list</h2>
      <p>Require both providers to explain the store origin, service-worker scope, subscription endpoint/token and provider credentials involved in a handover. A CSV of customers does not prove that the new provider can reach existing browsers. Inspect permitted, default and denied permission states and expired subscriptions; do not repeatedly prompt denied users. Get an explicit supported transfer or resubscription procedure before uninstalling scripts or changing service-worker ownership. Restrict token access and avoid exposing raw endpoints in routine reports. No automatic portability is assumed.</p>
      <h2>Prove continuity with a small controlled cohort</h2>
      <p>Record expected subscribers by browser/device and compare accepted subscriptions, reachable endpoints and actual receipt in that cohort. Include token renewal, unsubscribe, browser data clearing and a return visit after cutover. Inspect failures separately from delivery counts; an accepted job does not prove the notification was displayed or read. If subscriptions cannot transfer, quantify the reachable audience you would lose and plan a respectful new opt-in path. Keep the incumbent when that loss outweighs the proposed improvement.</p>
      <h2>Recovery handover needs one owner</h2>
      <p>Inventory scheduled cart reminders, stock alerts, welcome jobs and order notifications with IDs, event times and statuses. Stop old enrollments and cancel or reconcile queued work before the replacement owns a purpose. Recheck order, permission, suppression, stock and current price at send time; deduplicate late or replayed events. A purchase should stop its recovery sequence even if a delayed cart event arrives afterward. Coordinate push with email/SMS around the promised experience rather than imposing a universal twenty-four-hour rule. Assign support and any SMS reply/STOP/HELP responsibilities explicitly.</p>
      <h2>Preserve merchandising value without duplicate alerts</h2>
      <p>Separate gift purchases from personal replenishment, VIP early access from general discounts and genuine stock requests from broad broadcasts. Base replenishment timing on usable product/order evidence rather than an arbitrary calendar. Review quiet hours for the relevant channel and destinations, accessibility of prompts and supported browsers with the candidate. Track opt-outs and failures by journey alongside purchase quality, not just total subscriber count. This preserves useful recovery work while revealing which channel actually needs replacement.</p>
      <h2>Rollback includes subscriptions and queues</h2>
      <p>Keep a dated script and service-worker configuration record, exports and a restricted audit archive. Define the failure threshold and person allowed to revert before switching. Restore only the supported previous integration configuration, preserving new withdrawals and completed orders. Reconcile jobs created during the trial so rollback cannot send both providers’ recovery sequences. Do not assume deleted subscriptions or revoked credentials can be restored. If rollback requires renewed browser permission, treat that as a material migration cost before proceeding.</p>
      <h2>Compare ordinary and peak economics</h2>
      <p>Request matched quotes for the same eligible audience, destinations and sending volume. Include platform fees, connectors, implementation labor, SMS segments including Unicode, MMS and carrier charges where SMS is separately required. Push sends and SMS segments are different billing units. Record minimums, overages, registration, support and the cost of operating two systems. Compare refunds, opt-outs by flow, and repeat full-price purchases alongside attributed revenue; use a suitable holdout to estimate incremental orders.</p>
      <h2>Discounts need fixed-goods arithmetic</h2>
      <p>Illustrative assumptions, not merchant results: a $100 order with $45 goods cost and $10 other variable cost contributes $45 before messaging. A $25 discount leaves $20, keeping those costs fixed. Twenty incremental discounted orders contribute $400 before $300 campaign cost, leaving $100; thirty contribute $600 before $450 campaign cost, leaving $150. A discount alone does not prove a loss. Account for returns and purchases that would have happened anyway before claiming lift.</p>
      <p>Compare the separate handover in <a href="/alternatives/justuno-alternative">Justuno alternatives</a>: capture-field acceptance is different from browser subscription continuity.</p>
    </div>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Questions about Firepush alternatives" />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
